WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.260
Staging a complex stunt or a giant set piece
is a massive challenge.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.679
These achievements are incredible to watch,
but learning about what needed to happen behind

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:12.809
the scenes only makes us appreciate them more.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:17.700
Here's a look at some iconic movie scenes
that were nearly impossible to capture on

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.430
film.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.580
Fast Five

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:26.120
As quick-moving as its title suggests, Fast
Five is at its best during the climactic car

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.220
chase through the streets of Rio.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:33.540
Protagonists Dom and Brian have to drag a
100-ton bank vault behind them with the police

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.660
hot on their tail.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.940
[Tires squealing]

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:46.760
It's such an enormous set piece that it would
be totally understandable to assume it's mostly

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:47.770
CGI.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.950
Instead, it's nearly all practical effects.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:56.610
Director Justin Lin was adamant about using
as little CGI as possible, so stunt coordinator

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:01.690
Jack Gill built a replica vault that weighed
10,000 pounds.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:06.229
Although lighter than a real metal vault,
it still required souped-up cars with oversized

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:11.229
tires and 400-horsepower engines so that they
could actually swing around the replica.

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:14.659
When you're watching Dom and Brian swing a
vault through buildings and cement like a

00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:17.539
wrecking ball, that's actually happening.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.120
Somewhat surprisingly, no one was hurt in
the scene's filming.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.160
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.520
Mission: Impossible - Ghost Protocol

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.219
The Mission: Impossible franchise has mostly
become a series of films where we watch Tom

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.400
Cruise cheat death over and over.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.380
"I'm actually scared s---less"

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.299
But all other stunts pale in comparison next
to the tower-climbing sequence in Mission:

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:47.299
Impossible - Ghost Protocol.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:53.680
This sequence sees Cruise's Ethan Hunt climbing
the exterior of the Burj Khalifa, the tallest

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.020
building in the world.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.220
Cruise actually fired the film's insurance
company so he could perform the climb without

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.220
a stunt double, with only some rigging between
him and the ground.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:16.730
What's more, there was no way to cushion Cruise's
landing during the climactic jump back onto

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:17.400
the tower.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.480
[THUD]

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:31.080
He just went for it, landing full force on
the glass.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.700
Iron Man 3

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.360
Sometimes a well-performed practical effects
stunt actually looks like CGI because of how

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.010
impossible it seems.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:43.690
Case in point, the breathtaking airplane rescue
set piece from Iron Man 3.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.020
"Remember that game called Barrel of Monkeys?

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.680
That's what we're gonna do.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:48.680
18,000 feet.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.890
Come on people, everybody grab your monkey."

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.560
In order to shoot the scene without CGI, the
filmmakers began with two days of testing

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.340
at the airport to make sure it could even
work.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.390
Once they determined it was possible, they
proceeded to make the jump multiple times

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.480
a day all alongside a cameraman.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:11.930
"8 days to shoot it and we ended up making
62 airplane loads, over 600 parachute jumps,

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.690
480-some hidden parachute jumps."

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:20.790
The extra effort of using practical effects
resulted in a genuinely thrilling action sequence.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:21.790
Cliffhanger

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:28.570
1993's Cliffhanger is a perfect example of
a dangerous stunt making a movie a classic.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:33.340
One jaw-dropping scene involves a character
traveling from one airplane to another via

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.960
a single cable.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:40.220
The planes had to travel at an exact speed
of 150 miles per hour each.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.840
If either sped up, stuntman Simon Crane could
have been ripped in half.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:49.930
"Any faster, and the force of the wind would
likely pull Crane's limbs from his torso."

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:55.150
The altitude and wind created numbing temperatures
as well, with Crane having to don a special

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:58.260
prosthetic face mask in order to endure the
cold.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.540
Luckily, Crane managed to perform the stunt
almost perfectly, except for some trouble

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:04.330
getting pulled into the second plane.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:11.220
"I actually bounced into the door and bounced
out again and that's when I hit, went above,

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.860
and slid along the roof."

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:18.819
Crane's death-defying stunt is as breathtaking
now as it was when the film first came out.

00:04:18.819 --> 00:04:20.379
Ben-Hur

00:04:20.379 --> 00:04:25.009
Shooting a biblical drama in the 1950s offered
very little room for cinematic sleight of

00:04:25.009 --> 00:04:28.320
hand and 1959's Ben-Hur was no different.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.490
The famous chariot race took nearly a year
to prepare, with the million-dollar arena

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.979
set carved out of a quarry by a thousand workmen,
among other intense preparations.

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:41.629
"I was about to start shooting on Ben-Hur
in Rome.

00:04:41.629 --> 00:04:47.789
I went over about a month early to learn how
to drive a four-horse chariot."

00:04:47.789 --> 00:04:52.009
The shoot itself required nearly a hundred
trained racehorses that actually ended up

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:54.430
being faster than the car carrying the camera.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:59.009
This gave the cameramen only a few seconds
to get each shot per take, even with a head

00:04:59.009 --> 00:05:00.009
start.

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:04.520
In addition, the heat limited the horses to
eight runs a day, and a stunt gone wrong very

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:09.260
nearly resulted in stuntman Joe Canutt being
trampled by his horses and chariot.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.800
[Crowd cheering]

00:05:17.809 --> 00:05:22.039
Despite everything, the filmmakers and crew
managed to pull the whole thing off.

00:05:22.039 --> 00:05:27.300
For that alone, they deserved all 11 of the
Oscars the film received.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:28.990
Raiders of the Lost Ark

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:33.439
Indiana Jones and the Raiders of the Lost
Ark is chock full of some of the most memorable

00:05:33.439 --> 00:05:35.449
imagery in film history.

00:05:35.449 --> 00:05:40.030
One particularly famous stunt proved more
difficult than others: the sequence in which

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:44.990
Harrison Ford's stuntman, Terry Leonard, is
dragged along a dirt road behind a speeding

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:45.500
truck.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:58.660
Despite the fact that Leonard only drifts
under the truck for six seconds, the stunt

00:05:58.669 --> 00:06:00.350
was immensely dangerous.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:05.210
Thankfully, Leonard came away unscathed, pulling
off one of the greatest stunts of all time.

